<h2>Bhagwa pomegranate from India</h2>
<p>If you're importing Indian pomegranate, you're almost certainly buying Bhagwa. It's the variety that made India competitive in export markets: deep red arils, high sweetness, a thick skin that tolerates handling and long transits, and a shelf life of 45–60 days when the cold chain is managed properly.</p>

<p>We source from Maharashtra's Solapur, Sangli, Nashik, Pune and Ahmednagar belts, with supplementary volume from Gujarat, Karnataka and Rajasthan. Fruit is count-graded to your specification, sleeved, packed in 3.5kg or 4kg cartons and pre-cooled before loading into reefers set to 5–7°C.</p>

<h2>How should I specify pomegranate for my market?</h2>
<p>Two things matter more than anything else on a pomegranate order:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Count grade.</strong> Fruit is graded by weight — 200–250g, 250–300g, 300–400g, 400g+. Gulf buyers frequently want larger counts for retail display; some European programmes prefer mid-size fruit for consistency across a pack. Tell us your target and we'll quote against it rather than guessing.</li>
  <li><strong>Residue compliance.</strong> For EU and UK shipments, pesticide residue is the single most common cause of rejection across Indian fresh produce generally. We can arrange lab testing before shipment — <a href="/quality-compliance/">see our quality process</a> — but it needs to be agreed upfront, not after loading.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When is Indian pomegranate available?</h2>
<p>The main export season runs roughly September to March. Maharashtra growers stagger their bahar (flowering) cycles — ambe bahar, mrig bahar and hasta bahar — which is what stretches Indian supply across several months rather than concentrating it into one short window. Outside that main window, availability narrows and prices reflect it.</p>

<h2>Which markets buy Indian pomegranate?</h2>
<p>The UAE is India's single largest pomegranate destination by value, with Bangladesh and Nepal taking significant regional volume and the Netherlands acting as a major European entry point (from where fruit is redistributed across the EU). Saudi Arabia and other Gulf markets take steady volume through the season. The table below shows what the available data reports, along with its source, year and limitations.</p>
